BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:feminist-gaming-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: Feminist Gaming Fellowship: Open Call 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Feminist Gaming Project is opening applications for its fir
 st Fellowship cohort: five fellows from different creative and research fi
 elds spending three months developing 3D assets\, tools\, and documentatio
 n for its Open Access Asset Library (OAL)\, a free\, open repository of in
 clusive 3D content created by\, with\, and for intersectionally feminist a
 nd diverse communities. Where mainstream marketplaces default to hyper-ath
 letic\, idealized\, or hyper-sexualized characters\, the OAL offers averag
 e bodies\, real postures\, everyday objects\, and the kinds of lives most 
 games leave out (pregnancy and postpartum\, care work\, ageing\, disabilit
 y\, domestic labor). This first cohort is dedicated to 3D content. Support
 ed by the Creative Industries Fund NL and working across the Netherlands\,
  Türkiye\, Germany\, and the UK\, the project challenges exclusionary nor
 ms in how games are made. The fellowship offers a EUR 1\,000 honorarium pe
 r fellow over three months (half at start\, half on completion)\; mentorsh
 ip from the core team and advisors in feminist design\, game studies\, 3D 
 production\, and digital culture\; a peer community across NL\, TR\, DE\, 
 and the UK with biweekly meetings\; visibility through a travelling exhibi
 tion program\, publications\, and public events\; and authorship credited 
 in the OAL under its license terms. Five roles are recruited (one fellow e
 ach): 3D Character Artist for inclusive base meshes\; 3D Character Artist 
 for pregnancy and postpartum assets\; 3D Animator for neutral and everyday
  movement\; 3D Modeller for domestic and care-labor environments\; and Res
 earcher/Writer for documentation and the representation framework\; applic
 ants may rank up to two. Open to 3D artists\, animators\, modellers\, desi
 gners\, researchers\, and writers committed to inclusive and intersectiona
 l representation in games\; prior game-industry experience is not required
 \, and emerging creators\, underrepresented practitioners\, and researcher
 s at the intersection of games\, gender\, and culture are especially encou
 raged\, particularly those based in or connected to NL\, TR\, DE\, or the 
 UK. To apply\, email feministgamingproject@gmail.com with a short statemen
 t (max ~500 words)\, a portfolio or work samples (a link is fine)\, and co
 nfirmation of availability for the three-month period\; name the role(s) i
 n the subject line or statement. The project explicitly asks applicants to
  write statements in their own voice rather than using AI-generated text. 
 Indicative timeline: applications close 12 July 2026\; selection and notif
 ication late July\; fellowship period August to October 2026\; showcase an
 d publication November to December 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Feminist Gaming 
 Project\nLocation: Remote across the Netherlands\, Türkiye\, Germany\, an
 d the UK\nAward: EUR 1\,000 honorarium per fellow (paid half at the start\
 , half on completion)\nDuration: Three months\, August to October 2026\; m
 inimum ~6 hours per week (~75 hours total)\n\nMore: https://feministgaming
 .com/fellowship/index.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
URL:https://feministgaming.com/fellowship/index.html?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote across the Netherlands\, Türkiye\, Germany\, and the UK
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ddc-trygfonden-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Digital Democracy Centre x TrygFonden Fellowship Program 
 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Digital Democracy Centre (DDC) at the University of Souther
 n Denmark\, with support from TrygFonden\, is calling for applications for
  its 2027 fellowship cohort. The program fosters collaboration between res
 earchers\, journalists\, and policymakers exploring the impact of digital 
 technology and AI on media\, politics\, and democracy. Fellows collaborate
  with a self-selected DDC mentor to generate new knowledge\, contribute to
  public debate\, develop networks\, and produce tangible outreach outputs 
 such as policy briefs\, educational packages\, and talks. THREE FELLOWSHIP
  TYPES: (1) academic fellows researching digital technologies and democrac
 y\; (2) journalist fellows covering digital technology and democracy\; (3)
  policy fellows contributing to the regulatory debate. Target group: resea
 rchers\, journalism graduates\, and policymakers in research/educational i
 nstitutions\, media organizations\, ministries\, NGOs\, etc. PRIORITIES: p
 rojects with clear relevance to Denmark or Danish society (including compa
 rative/international projects yielding insights relevant to a Danish conte
 xt)\; in 2027\, projects related to DDC's Hybrid Warfare Initiative (socie
 tal resilience to new forms of international conflict\, with a focus on di
 gital technologies and AI) are also prioritized. EXPECTATIONS: applicants 
 must secure confirmation of mentorship from their desired DDC mentor BEFOR
 E applying (applications without a confirmed mentor are automatically reje
 cted\; PhD students and research assistants are generally not eligible as 
 mentors\, and Sten Schaumburg-Muller is unavailable as a mentor for the 20
 27 call)\; applicants must also coordinate support from their current supe
 rvisors/managers. Fellows engage in DDC activities\, seminars/workshops\, 
 and public outreach\, and submit a short report within two months of finis
 hing. APPLICATION (in English): preliminary project title\, fellowship typ
 e\, brief project description (max 1000 chars)\, detailed description and 
 timeline (max 2400 chars\, including duration\, confirmed mentor\, and rem
 ote/in-office work plan)\, description of activities and collaboration wit
 h external practice partners (max 1000 chars)\, expected product/impact/ou
 tput (max 500 chars)\, a CV (max 5 pages)\, and an overview of the posts f
 or which financial support is sought. Submit the application form (PDF or 
 Word) plus CV as PDFs to ddc@sdu.dk with subject line 'DDC / TrygFonden Fe
 llowship application'. Deadline 1 September 2026\; decisions communicated 
 no later than 1 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Digital Democracy Centre (U
 niversity of Southern Denmark)\, with support from TrygFonden\nLocation: O
 dense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of in-offic
 e stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned to the Dan
 ish working calendar.\nAward: Either a salary subsidy or a stipend to help
  with travel and/or housing\; specific benefits (financial support\, acces
 s to DDC research resources\, flexible working spaces\, speaker series\, n
 etworks) vary by agreement between the fellow\, DDC\, and partnering organ
 izations. Applicants describe the posts they seek support for (flights\, h
 ousing\, research costs\, salary compensation\, stipend\, etc.).\nDuration
 : Flexible\; typically 2-6 months (minimum two months\, can include shorte
 r DDC stays and remote periods)\, longer in exceptional cases. Fellowships
  start anywhere in 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forsknin
 gsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracycentre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowshi
 p-call-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forskningsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracy
 centre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowship-call-2027?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Odense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of
  in-office stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned t
 o the Danish working calendar.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tba21-academy-tentacular-fellowship-ocean-space-venice-2026-2027@artifi
 cialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260706
SUMMARY:Deadline: TBA21-Academy: Tentacular Fellowship 2026-2027 (Ocean Spa
 ce\, Venice)
DESCRIPTION:TBA21-Academy\, in collaboration with NICHE Centre for Environm
 ental Humanities and the UNESCO Chair on Water\, Heritage and Sustainable 
 Development at Ca' Foscari University\, is launching the first edition of 
 the Tentacular Fellowship\, an initiative-building fellowship based at Oce
 an Space and at Ca' Foscari in Venice. Conceived as a long-term platform f
 or artistic research and ecological engagement\, Ocean Space works to deve
 lop new forms of action capable of moving beyond awareness-raising to prod
 uce real\, tangible territorial effects through situated collaboration wit
 h the communities\, practitioners\, researchers and civic actors already w
 orking within the Lagoon of Venice. The fellowship invites a practitioner 
 or collective to initiate\, consolidate or expand a situated initiative in
  the Lagoon through the creation of a Tentacular Unit: a collaborative\, i
 nterdisciplinary platform able to gather artists\, students\, researchers\
 , institutions\, local communities and other actors around a specific soci
 o-environmental challenge. Through situated research\, alliance-building a
 nd public-facing formats\, the fellow helps build the social\, institution
 al and ecological conditions for the initiative to take root and grow over
  time. STRUCTURE: the two-year fellowship runs in two phases. In Phase 1 (
 incubation) the fellow is awarded a EUR 3\,000 grant to support independen
 t research and initiative development. In Phase 2 (activation) the fellow 
 is awarded a EUR 8\,000 grant plus a EUR 15\,000 activation budget\, for t
 otal fellowship support of EUR 26\,000. Details on the application process
 \, evaluation criteria and frequently asked questions are in the full open
 -call document. Application deadline: 5 July 2026\, 11:59pm CET. Apply via
  the online form linked from the open-call page.\n\nOrganisation: TBA21-Ac
 ademy\, with NICHE Centre for Environmental Humanities and the UNESCO Chai
 r on Water\, Heritage and Sustainable Development at Ca' Foscari Universit
 y\nLocation: Ocean Space and Ca' Foscari University\, Venice\, Italy\; sit
 uated work in and with the communities of the Lagoon of Venice.\nAward: To
 tal fellowship support EUR 26\,000. Phase 1 (incubation): EUR 3\,000 grant
  for independent research and initiative development. Phase 2 (activation)
 : EUR 8\,000 grant plus a EUR 15\,000 activation budget. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: 15 months\, September 2026 to November 2027\n\nMore: https:/
 /tba21.org/oceanspace-research-tentacular-fellowship?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://tba21.org/oceanspace-research-tentacular-fellowship?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ocean Space and Ca' Foscari University\, Venice\, Italy\; situated
  work in and with the communities of the Lagoon of Venice.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Foundation: Fellowship 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:Founded by the artist and philanthropist Jerome Hill\, the Cama
 rgo Foundation has run this residency in Cassis on the Mediterranean coast
  since 1971\, and more than 1\,500 fellows have been through it. Fourteen 
 fellowships are awarded per cycle\, split roughly seven artists and seven 
 scholars or thinkers\, which is the point: the interdisciplinary cohort is
  the programme\, not a side effect. IMPORTANT: the Foundation has stated t
 hat this is the FINAL fellowship awarded through a public open call before
  it moves to a new selection process from 2027\, so if you have been meani
 ng to apply\, this is the cycle. RESEARCH\, NOT PRODUCTION: this is delibe
 rately structured as a research residency. You propose a project or line o
 f inquiry\, which can be development of a new work\, research preceding fu
 ture creative work\, or a broader investigation in your practice. Visual a
 rtists are explicitly reminded that research is prioritised over productio
 n\, and the Foundation supplies no art materials\, technical equipment or 
 artwork shipping\, so anything needing extensive fabrication is a poor fit
 . Fellows lead one resident session built around their own research or met
 hodology\, and attendance at these sessions is mandatory for everyone. Opt
 ional public Open Studios and introductions to organisations across the Ai
 x-Marseille-Provence region are also part of it. ELIGIBILITY: professional
  artists\, scholars and thinkers of any nationality. Artists should have a
 t least five years of professional practice and a mature body of work evid
 enced by exhibitions\, performances\, publications\, awards or grants. Per
 formance artists\, theatre directors\, choreographers\, multidisciplinary 
 artists\, writers\, filmmakers\, composers and visual artists are all elig
 ible within their categories. Collaborative applications of up to three me
 mbers are accepted. APPLICATION: four separate written responses covering 
 the project\, the research questions\, why Camargo specifically\, and why 
 now in your career\; reviewers are reading for a coherent research traject
 ory rather than a production proposal. An applicant webinar runs 1 Septemb
 er 2026. Deadline 1 October 2026 at 17:59 EST / 23:59 CET\; notifications 
 April 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Fondation Camargo\nLocation: Cassis\, France 
 (Mediterranean coast)\nAward: EUR 3\,500 stipend (EUR 350 per week for 10 
 weeks)\, basic transportation to and from Cassis\, a furnished apartment o
 n campus\, and a studio or appropriate workspace\, plus a reference librar
 y\, music and conference room and open-air theatre. Art materials\, techni
 cal equipment and shipping of artworks are NOT provided. No application fe
 e listed.\nDuration: Ten weeks. Fall 2027 session: 8 September to 17 Novem
 ber 2027. Spring 2028 session: 23 February to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https:/
 /camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cassis\, France (Mediterranean coast)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cais-research-sabbaticals-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260723
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260724
SUMMARY:Deadline: CAIS Research Sabbaticals (Fellowships) 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:Residential research sabbaticals at CAIS\, explicitly open to e
 xcellent scholars AND practitioners across all career stages and disciplin
 es (not academia-only). Funds individual projects on the societal impact o
 f digital transformation\, including pure research and applied projects th
 at develop new theories\, methods or perspectives for practice. Project mu
 st be self-contained with specific milestones and produce an independent o
 utput suitable for short-timeframe publication: peer-reviewed paper or con
 ference contribution\, book chapter\, policy paper\, or prototype. Fellows
  join a vibrant interdisciplinary research community with regular joint ac
 tivities (breakfast Tuesdays\, colloquium and dinner Wednesdays\, occasion
 al workshops Thursdays) and an international network of alumni\, working g
 roups and affiliates. In Germany\, full and compensatory grants are not su
 bject to social security contributions and are usually tax-exempt\; fellow
 s resident abroad should verify their own tax position. Note: CAIS is curr
 ently reviewing the application format and selection process for the next 
 call\, so details may change. Next call publishes at the beginning of June
  2026\; deadline 23 July 2026 for fellowships in the period October 2027 t
 o March 2028. Contact: Dr. Esther Laufer\, esther.laufer@cais-research.de.
 \n\nOrganisation: Center for Advanced Internet Studies (CAIS)\, Bochum\, G
 ermany\nLocation: Bochum\, Germany (residential at CAIS\; rent-free\, full
 y furnished apartment provided plus private office)\nAward: Sabbatical lea
 ve on full salary: additional €600/month grant. No regular income: €2\
 ,000/month grant. Regular income below €1\,400/month: top-up to the full
  grant. Alternative: reimbursement of salary or substitute costs within re
 asonable limits. €100/month extra per child under 18 for fellows on a fu
 ll or compensatory grant. CAIS also covers one return trip to Bochum (or a
  daily commute if local)\, provides a rent-free fully furnished apartment 
 and a private office. Fellows can request financial support for research e
 xpenses\, invite a Visiting Fellow for up to 3 weeks of collaboration\, an
 d invite up to three European experts for half-day workshops (CAIS covers 
 travel\, accommodation and a daily allowance of up to €24).\nDuration: 3
  or 6 months. 6-month fellowships usually start in October or April\; 3-mo
 nth fellowships start in October\, January\, April or July. Exceptions pos
 sible by arrangement.\n\nMore: https://www.cais-research.de/en/cais-colleg
 e/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.cais-research.de/en/cais-college/fellowships/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Bochum\, Germany (residential at CAIS\; rent-free\, fully furnishe
 d apartment provided plus private office)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:msca-postdoctoral-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: Marie Skłodowska-Curie Postdoctoral Fellowships 2026 (MS
 CA-PF)
DESCRIPTION:MSCA Postdoctoral Fellowships support researchers holding a PhD
  who wish to carry out research abroad\, acquire new skills\, develop thei
 r careers and have international mobility. Open to excellent researchers o
 f any nationality. The 2026 call opened 9 April 2026 and closes 9 Septembe
 r 2026 at 17:00 CEST\; notification of call results expected February 2027
 \; grant agreement signature April 2027. Two strands: European Postdoctora
 l Fellowships (12 to 24 months in Europe) and Global Postdoctoral Fellowsh
 ips (12 to 24 months outside Europe + 12-month return phase in Europe). Th
 e project must take place in a country different from where the researcher
  has worked or studied. Approximately 1\,600 projects funded. Apply via th
 e EU Funding & Tenders Opportunities Portal\; submission is by the fellow 
 plus host institution. Eligibility: researcher must have a PhD at the call
  deadline (or have submitted thesis with all requirements met)\, maximum 8
  years full-time-equivalent research experience post-PhD\, must comply wit
 h the mobility rule (no more than 12 months in the host country in the 36 
 months before the deadline).\n\nOrganisation: European Commission (Marie S
 kłodowska-Curie Actions\, under Horizon Europe\; managed by the European 
 Research Executive Agency\, REA)\nLocation: Europe (host institution in an
  EU Member State or Horizon Europe Associated Country\; also possible: Glo
 bal Postdoctoral Fellowships hosted in non-associated third countries with
  a return phase in Europe)\nAward: Standard MSCA unit costs covering resea
 rcher allowance (living\, mobility\, family)\, research/training/networkin
 g costs and management/indirect costs (annual values published in the call
 's Work Programme\; typically a fully-funded postdoc package of roughly 
 €5\,000 to €8\,000+ per researcher per month equivalent depending on h
 ost country correction coefficient)\nDuration: 12 to 24 months (European P
 ostdoctoral Fellowships)\; Global Postdoctoral Fellowships are 24 to 36 mo
 nths including a 12-month return phase in Europe\n\nMore: https://marie-sk
 lodowska-curie-actions.ec.europa.eu/funding/msca-postdoctoral-fellowships-
 2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://marie-sklodowska-curie-actions.ec.europa.eu/funding/msca-postdo
 ctoral-fellowships-2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
LOCATION:Europe (host institution in an EU Member State or Horizon Europe A
 ssociated Country\; also possible: Global Postdoctoral Fellowships hosted 
 in non-associated third countries with a return phase in Europe)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:canon-foundation-europe-research-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Canon Foundation in Europe Research Fellowships 2026 (Eur
 ope–Japan Exchange)
DESCRIPTION:Canon Foundation in Europe Research Fellowships support bilater
 al research exchange between Europe and Japan\, awarding up to 15 fellowsh
 ips annually. AMOUNT: up to EUR 30\,000 per year (pro-rated for shorter st
 ays)\, flexible — covers living costs\, travel\, insurance\, research ex
 penses\, and books\; fellows determine their own allocation. ELIGIBILITY: 
 European nationals (including UK\, Israel\, Switzerland\, Turkey\, Balkan 
 and Baltic countries) conducting research in Japan\; or Japanese nationals
  conducting research in Europe. Applicants must have completed at least a 
 Master's or PhD degree within the last 10 years (older qualifications may 
 apply with supporting documentation). No restrictions on research field. P
 riority given to first-time applicants to Europe/Japan. Fellows arrange th
 eir own host institution and advisor. Up to 15 fellowships awarded annuall
 y. Deadline: 15 September 2026\, 23:59 CET (for fellowships starting Janua
 ry-December 2027). Apply at https://portal.canonfoundation.org/. Contact: 
 canon.foundation@canon-europe.com.\n\nOrganisation: Canon Foundation in Eu
 rope\nLocation: Europe or Japan (bilateral exchange\; fellows arrange own 
 host institution)\nAward: Up to EUR 30\,000 per year (pro-rated for shorte
 r stays). No application fee.\nDuration: Minimum 3 months\, maximum 1 year
 . Fellowships for 2027 start.\n\nMore: https://portal.canonfoundation.org/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://portal.canonfoundation.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe or Japan (bilateral exchange\; fellows arrange own host ins
 titution)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:imera-postdoctoral-residency-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260704
SUMMARY:Deadline: Iméra: 'This Is Not Another Fellowship' – Postdoctoral
  Residency 2026-27 (Aix-Marseille)
DESCRIPTION:Iméra's 'This Is Not Another Fellowship' is a 3-month postdoct
 oral residency at the Institute for Advanced Study at Aix Marseille Univer
 sité\, offered in two semester options: November 2\, 2026 – January 26\
 , 2027\, or March 1\, 2027 – May 28\, 2027. PROVIDED: free accommodation
  in one of fourteen campus apartments\; travel expenses covered\; desk in 
 shared open co-working space\; participation in the weekly Community Build
 ing Seminar (mandatory 3-hour Thursday sessions)\; access to interdiscipli
 nary scholarly networks. ELIGIBILITY: under 35 years old by May 28\, 2027\
 ; currently holding a postdoctoral fellowship contract at a university\; h
 ave not lived in France for more than 12 months in the prior 3 years\; if 
 requiring a visa\, must demonstrate sufficient funds. Research must align 
 with one of four Iméra programs: Arts & Sciences: Indisciplined Knowledge
 \; Interdisciplinary Dialogues\; Mediterranean\; or Necessary Utopias. All
  disciplines welcome. DEADLINE: 3 July 2026 at 1pm (CET+2). Apply at https
 ://www.imera.fr/en/this-is-not-another-fellowship-application-2026-27/.\n\
 nOrganisation: Iméra – Institute for Advanced Study at Aix Marseille Un
 iversité\nLocation: Aix-Marseille\, France (campus residency)\nAward: Fre
 e accommodation in campus apartment\; travel expenses covered\; office in 
 co-working space. No cash stipend specified. No application fee.\nDuration
 : 3 months: either November 2\, 2026 – January 26\, 2027\, or March 1\, 
 2027 – May 28\, 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.imera.fr/en/this-is-not-anoth
 er-fellowship-application-2026-27/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.imera.fr/en/this-is-not-another-fellowship-application-2026
 -27/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Aix-Marseille\, France (campus residency)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kb-researcher-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T204343Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260927
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260928
SUMMARY:Deadline: KB Researcher-in-Residence (RiR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:ELIGIBILITY: two Researcher-in-Residence positions in 2027. Ope
 n to early-career researchers of any discipline (data science\, social sci
 ence\, computer science\, humanities) who are currently working on a PhD o
 r within 5 years of their PhD end date. You must have\, or be able to secu
 re\, an affiliation with a Dutch university or research institute willing 
 to second you\; external PhD students may use the funding to obtain such a
  contract. Your project must make use of one or more of the KB digital col
 lections and you must be able to work with primarily Dutch data. You must 
 be available at 0.5 FTE over 6 months and able to spend two days a week on
 -site at the KB in The Hague. COLLECTIONS: over 130 million digitized page
 s of newspapers\, magazines\, radio bulletins and books from 1470 onward (
 via Delpher and DBNL)\, a born-digital web collection of more than 23\,000
  websites\, ground-truth and derived datasets on the KB Lab\, and metadata
  including Linked Data. APPLY: use the official proposal template and subm
 it a single PDF by 27 September 2026 to dh@kb.nl\, after reading the call'
 s terms and conditions\; questions to dh@kb.nl. TIMELINE: deadline 27 Sept
 ember 2026\; internal eligibility review followed by an external committee
  review\; outcome notified November 2026\; residencies start Q1 and Q3 202
 7. DISCIPLINES: digital humanities / data science / computational research
  with cultural-heritage collections. NOTE: the output is usually a dataset
  or tool prototype hosted on the KB Lab\; you agree to share results via b
 log posts on the KB Lab and to publish resulting tools or data under an op
 en license within 6 months of the residency. Proposals should align with t
 he KB Research Agenda or the Cultural AI Lab.\n\nOrganisation: KB\, Nation
 al Library of the Netherlands\nLocation: The Hague\, Netherlands (two days
  per week on-site at the KB premises)\nAward: Secondment at 0.5 FTE for 6 
 months based on your current salary\, up to EUR 5\,590 gross per month for
  a full-time-equivalent position\, plus travel expenses\, office facilitie
 s\, and support from a research software engineer\, research advisor\, and
  collection and data specialists. No application fee.\nDuration: 6 months 
 at 0.5 FTE (two days per week on-site)\; two positions in 2027\, starting 
 Q1 and Q3\n\nMore: https://lab.kb.nl/news/call-kb-researcher-residence-rir
 -2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://lab.kb.nl/news/call-kb-researcher-residence-rir-2027?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:The Hague\, Netherlands (two days per week on-site at the KB premi
 ses)
END:VEVENT
